106 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (Silicone is different from standard 3D printing materials. It can't be melted down like plastic filament, and pure silicone doesn't harden when exposed to UV light. Because of this, it cannot be printed on a normal 3D printer. Instead, companies have created special machines and methods to work around these challenges.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
107 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (These new processes allow liquid or paste-like silicone to be laid down layer by layer and then solidified using heat, specialized UV formulas, or chemical reactions. This makes it possible to build flexible, rubber-like parts directly from a digital file for the first time.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
108 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (Processes include:)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
