115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (This method, also known as Liquid Additive Manufacturing \(LAM\), functions by extruding a thick, paste-like silicone compound. The material is pushed through a nozzle and deposited onto a build platform, tracing the shape of each layer. The process is repeated, using a bottom up approach to build the part.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (To solidify the material, heat is applied. Many systems use an integrated heating element or a halogen lamp. This heat triggers a chemical reaction within the silicone, causing it to cross-link and change from a paste into a solid, elastic rubber. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
117 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (A leading example of this technology is the InnovatiQ LiQ 320 printer. This system uses a specialized Dow Chemical silicone formulation and can produce finished, functional parts. An optional coloring system allows for parts to be made in different colors during the printing process itself. This approach is well-suited for creating medical device parts with personalization.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
