123 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (Drop-on-Demand printing operates with high precision, similar to an inkjet printer but using silicone. The printhead releases individual, microscopic droplets of a liquid silicone mixture onto the build area. Each droplet is precisely placed to form a thin layer of the part.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
124 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (After a layer is completed, a UV light passes over it. This light activates a special agent mixed into the silicone, starting the curing process and hardening the material. The printer then deposits the next layer on top. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
125 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (A representative machine using this method is the Lynxter S300X. It features two independent extruders: one for printing the silicone part material and a separate one for printing a dissolvable support material, which is needed for creating complex overhanging geometries. This technology can print with a range of medical and industrial-grade silicones. It is important to note that it discontinued its development in 2021.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
